# Machine stage-reports that /pharn-dev-regress and /pharn-dev-verify must write VERBATIM — each is its
# floor helper's stdout JSON, deliberately NOT formatted so the recorded verdict is the helper's own
# bytes. Without these entries those two requirements conflict with whole-repo `format:check`, which the
# SAME commands run as a verdict-owning gate — but only when an array is non-empty: `JSON.stringify(…,
# null, 2)` expands `"failing_gates": ["test"]` across three lines where prettier wants it inline. Every
# run that reached those steps with a PASS had `[]`, on which the two agree, so the conflict sat latent
# until the first FAIL — at which point the stage's own artifact reddened the gate the stage owns.
# The product twins are listed too: same requirement, same latency, and no reason to wait for the first
# product-pipeline FAIL to discover it.
.dev/features/*/verify-report.json
.dev/features/*/regression-report.json
pharn/features/*/verify-report.json
pharn/features/*/regression-report.json

# cost.json — the SAME requirement as the two above, listed on the same reasoning rather than after the
# first FAIL discovers it (L23). `pharn/floor/render-cost-ledger.mjs` writes the emitter's own bytes, and
# `check-cost-ledger.mjs` recomputes every view from `requests[]`, so a formatter re-wrapping the file is
# a second writer of an artifact one tool is supposed to own. It is also large — ~393 KiB for a single
# run — so reformatting it would be a standing cost in every `format:check`.
pharn/features/*/cost.json

# RUN-REPORT.md — the SAME requirement as cost.json above, listed on the same reasoning rather than
# after the first FAIL discovers it (L23). `pharn/floor/render-run-report.mjs` renders it
# deterministically, and a formatter re-wrapping it would be a second writer of an artifact one tool
# owns. Sharper here than for cost.json: the report QUOTES untrusted text verbatim — Handoff bodies and
# PLAN `## Files` lines it does not control — so gate-clean output is not achievable by construction,
# and the conflict would land on `/pharn-verify`, a gate `/pharn-loop` itself owns.
pharn/features/*/RUN-REPORT.md

# REGRESSION.md — the SAME reasoning as RUN-REPORT.md above (L23, stage-regress-script 6.23.0):
# `pharn/floor/render-regression.mjs` renders it deterministically FROM `stage-regress.mjs`, and it
# quotes a shelled checker's own message and a hostile gate id verbatim — content the renderer does not
# control — so gate-clean output is not achievable by construction. Listed on reasoning, not after the
# first FAIL.
pharn/features/*/REGRESSION.md

# VERIFY.md — the SAME reasoning as REGRESSION.md above (L23, stage-verify-script 6.24.0):
# `pharn/floor/render-verify.mjs` renders it deterministically FROM `pharn/floor/stage-verify.mjs`, and it quotes
# gate ids, PLAN-derived paths and a checker's own message verbatim — content the renderer does not control — so
# gate-clean output is not achievable by construction, and the conflict would land on `/pharn-verify` itself.
pharn/features/*/VERIFY.md

# The other machine-written JSON pipeline artifacts, on the SAME reasoning (L23): each is one floor module's
# `JSON.stringify(…, null, 2)` output, and that layout expands a short array of strings over several lines where
# prettier wants it inline. AC-TESTS.lock.json (ac-tests-lock.mjs) is written by /pharn-test BEFORE the build and
# outside its scope, so a test-first dogfood run here failed its own `format:check` at verify, and a lock committed
# by /pharn-loop Step 6c reddened `npm run check` (review finding, 6.20.5). assignments.json
# (render-review-assignments.mjs) fails the same way; findings.json (merge-findings.mjs, objects only) passes today
# and is listed before a primitive array makes it fail. check-regress.test.mjs's ★ enumeration requires every
# machine-written `.json` member of PIPELINE_ARTIFACTS to appear here.
pharn/features/*/AC-TESTS.lock.json
pharn/features/*/assignments.json
pharn/features/*/findings.json

